  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "ACE inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs) are equally recommended as first-line medications for high blood pressure, but ARBs offer a safer side-effect profile.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "ARBs offer a safer side-effect profile"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Effectiveness in Lowering Blood Pressure"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Equal blood pressure control: Both drug classes lower blood pressure effectively by relaxing your blood vessels."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Similar heart and kidney protection: Large studies show no significant difference between ACE inhibitors and ARBs in preventing heart attacks, strokes, or overall heart events."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Equal guideline status: Major health organizations rank both classes as top-tier choices for initial therapy, detailed further in the American Heart Association Study .",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "American Heart Association Study",
                "link": "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/HYPERTENSIONAHA.120.16667"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Side Effects and Tolerability"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Dry cough: ACE inhibitors often cause an annoying, dry cough because they change a chemical in the body called bradykinin. ARBs do not affect bradykinin and rarely cause a cough."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Angioedema: ACE inhibitors carry a higher risk of angioedema, which is a dangerous swelling of the lips, tongue, or throat. ARBs have a much lower risk of this reaction."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Patient drop-out rates: Because ARBs cause fewer bothersome side effects, patients are slightly less likely to stop taking them compared to ACE inhibitors, as supported by the Cochrane Systematic Review."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Important Safety Rules"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "No pregnancy: Neither ACE inhibitors nor ARBs should be used during pregnancy because they can harm an unborn baby."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Never combine them: You should never take an ACE inhibitor and an ARB at the same time, as the combination increases risks like high potassium and kidney damage without adding extra heart benefits."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you are deciding between these medications, please tell me:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Are you currently taking one of these drugs and experiencing a dry cough ?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Do you have other conditions like diabetes or kidney disease?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help you prepare questions to discuss with your doctor."
      }
    ],
